{"title":"2021 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 21","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Diagram\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Diagram</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(Solution_1_But_More_Condensed)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (Solution 1 But More Condensed)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_OmegaLearn_(Angle_Chasing_and_Equilateral_Triangles)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by OmegaLearn (Angle Chasing and Equilateral Triangles)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_TheBeautyofMath\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by TheBeautyofMath</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/0/2/8029902caf855d940e465d04ff40101d8850c3e0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCDEF$\" width=\"86\" height=\"13\" /> be an equiangular hexagon. The lines <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/b/26b5cce32b3ba0fff61b9ce3db1da0146e9901d8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB, CD,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"70\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/2/c128243ea8125d0bb007b8132dcfa44461df73a7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EF$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> determine a triangle with area <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/4/fe45e92ea72cdab15e186a2a5287a906fab52cc6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$192\\sqrt{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"52\" height=\"18\" />, and the lines <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/c/a4c4423fa03c28a09062170b6ba5cbc6b64d96f7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC, DE,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"73\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/3/193c5caa0ee1174e95025208873f5495493a2059.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$FA$\" width=\"27\" height=\"13\" /> determine a triangle with area <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/b/92bd41cebf35cdd035b945481bb3925afc55e9fd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$324\\sqrt{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"52\" height=\"18\" />. The perimeter of hexagon <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/0/2/8029902caf855d940e465d04ff40101d8850c3e0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCDEF$\" width=\"86\" height=\"13\" /> can be expressed as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/0/6/8063945efc80d504432f1ade6b77d4b899040215.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m +n\\sqrt{p}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"73\" height=\"18\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/f/4/5f4bf5eb49eb976370c998db03e26d528d21dd3e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m, n,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"39\" height=\"11\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> are positive integers and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> is not divisible by the square of any prime. What is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/2/8f23423034093484df72a3171da00744b1e11963.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m + n + p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"80\" height=\"14\" />?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/9/8d9ccc7cb043d1aa5c98b3a715e3e3fe6eb542ce.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)} ~47\\qquad\\textbf{(B)} ~52\\qquad\\textbf{(C)} ~55\\qquad\\textbf{(D)} ~58\\qquad\\textbf{(E)} ~63$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"394\" height=\"17\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Diagram\">Diagram</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Diagram\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/3/05389489ce641e59cc3940c005f2b3ca657607bd.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] /* Made by MRENTHUSIASM */ size(250); path P1, P2; P1 = scale(16sqrt(3))*polygon(3); P2 = shift(3,3)*scale(36)*rotate(180)*polygon(3); draw(P1, dashed+black); draw(P2, dashed+black); pair A, B, C, D, E, F; E = intersectionpoints(P1,P2)[0]; F = intersectionpoints(P1,P2)[1]; A = intersectionpoints(P1,P2)[2]; B = intersectionpoints(P1,P2)[3]; C = intersectionpoints(P1,P2)[4]; D = intersectionpoints(P1,P2)[5]; filldraw(A--B--C--D--E--F--cycle,yellow); dot(&quot;$E$&quot;,E,1.5*dir(0),linewidth(4)); dot(&quot;$F$&quot;,F,1.5*dir(60),linewidth(4)); dot(&quot;$A$&quot;,A,1.5*dir(120),linewidth(4)); dot(&quot;$B$&quot;,B,1.5*dir(180),linewidth(4)); dot(&quot;$C$&quot;,C,1.5*dir(-120),linewidth(4)); dot(&quot;$D$&quot;,D,1.5*dir(-60),linewidth(4)); dot(16sqrt(3)*dir(90)^^16sqrt(3)*dir(210)^^16sqrt(3)*dir(330),linewidth(4)); dot((3,3)+36*dir(30)^^(3,3)+36*dir(150)^^(3,3)+36*dir(270),linewidth(4)); [/asy]\" width=\"418\" height=\"408\" />\n~MRENTHUSIASM\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/f/21fbd1066ec981b0e6726b58fbbfecb6600411aa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P,Q,R,X,Y,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"107\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/a/3ead47fb9fb4a4c273feee398f72ff2a09702b84.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Z$\" width=\"13\" height=\"12\" /> be the intersections <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/4/ab4e98152e8d7cdff30e1d448c796c2bf0dc9604.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overleftrightarrow{AB}\\cap\\overleftrightarrow{CD},\\overleftrightarrow{CD}\\cap\\overleftrightarrow{EF},\\overleftrightarrow{EF}\\cap\\overleftrightarrow{AB},\\overleftrightarrow{BC}\\cap\\overleftrightarrow{DE},\\overleftrightarrow{DE}\\cap\\overleftrightarrow{FA},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"431\" height=\"24\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/4/784cb64f62ae625be42a43c6115ce0e1d6f8572c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overleftrightarrow{FA}\\cap\\overleftrightarrow{BC},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"84\" height=\"24\" /> respectively.\n</p><p>The sum of the interior angles of any hexagon is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/f/dcffd42e375e1574d82f8f9d019a13abc9d0d27f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$720^\\circ.$\" width=\"38\" height=\"13\" /> Since hexagon <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/0/2/8029902caf855d940e465d04ff40101d8850c3e0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCDEF$\" width=\"86\" height=\"13\" /> is equiangular, each of its interior angles is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/9/2c9f36a46476f04d37cc92446c1286914e82e8a6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$720^\\circ\\div6=120^\\circ.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"132\" height=\"16\" /> By angle chasing, we conclude that the interior angles of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/d/a0da861fbbfc24db3f7071eaa81112759818e924.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle PBC,\\triangle QDE,\\triangle RFA,\\triangle XCD,\\triangle YEF,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"336\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/8/b18faaad54ca91709598db459a1f49491f9ed08d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ZAB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"60\" height=\"18\" /> are all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/c/d7c2c21e4d6e19c4a002d68ad75ba8942318b14d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$60^\\circ.$\" width=\"29\" height=\"13\" /> Therefore, these triangles are all equilateral triangles, from which <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/4/a/b4a455d533200d7dfbd00731fa8bd9e8796fb803.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle PQR$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/3/3039f01ba9e41f5490b7130e344be554015a25b9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle XYZ$\" width=\"60\" height=\"13\" /> are both equilateral triangles.\n</p><p>We are given that\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/f/5/5f5b55f5b4fa69ea2c5266b0c2cb7fc1f0ca9f17.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{alignat*}{8} [PQR]&amp;=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\cdot PQ^2&amp;&amp;=192\\sqrt3, \\\\ [XYZ]&amp;=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\cdot YZ^2&amp;&amp;=324\\sqrt3, \\end{alignat*}\" width=\"234\" height=\"88\" />\nso we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/f/cdf53d9765ec8d47166bc42e47eceba71b72feef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PQ=16\\sqrt3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"97\" height=\"23\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/b/a7ba3be640b89c1adc4eefbf47b76f445c225071.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$YZ=36,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"78\" height=\"18\" /> respectively.\n</p><p>By equilateral triangles and segment addition, we find the perimeter of hexagon <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/5/6f56dd7984513e66daf1136818700f6ce3098d5e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCDEF:$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"99\" height=\"14\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/5/365eff60f48d230c4d5872a99c6da83a9eea73ea.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} AB+BC+CD+DE+EF+FA&amp;=AZ+PC+CD+DQ+YF+FA \\\\ &amp;=(YF+FA+AZ)+(PC+CD+DQ) \\\\ &amp;=YZ+PQ \\\\ &amp;=36+16\\sqrt{3}. \\end{align*}\" width=\"619\" height=\"94\" />\nFinally, the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/8/7185aab1c2f20ffe6ce13eda715af78f61d1543b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$36+16+3=\\boxed{\\textbf{(C)} ~55}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"185\" height=\"30\" />\n</p><p>~sugar_rush ~MRENTHUSIASM\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let the length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/b/bab02463fc79717d7de8bb36c59ce089d580908d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB=x, BC=y.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"141\" height=\"18\" /> Then, we have\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/9/989a538880d178b905e4370f222c6568f189fa1c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} (y+2x)^2\\cdot\\frac{\\sqrt 3}{4}&amp;=324\\sqrt3, \\\\ (x+2y)^2\\cdot\\frac{\\sqrt 3}{4}&amp;=192\\sqrt3. \\end{align*}\" width=\"192\" height=\"88\" />\nWe get\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/a/6da63e9af209537603dabd068625ab5ef002ef14.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} y+2x&amp;=36, \\\\ x+2y&amp;=16\\sqrt3. \\end{align*}\" width=\"121\" height=\"45\" />\nWe want <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/1/6f10be13e75299920d03bf9b6f8674c1b220fcf1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3x+3y,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"67\" height=\"17\" /> and it follows that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/2/9/a29c06b74fd9be93c7465483cdcedea6323263c0.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[3x+3y=(y+2x)+(x+2y)=36+16\\sqrt3.\\]\" width=\"348\" height=\"21\" />\nFinally, the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/8/7185aab1c2f20ffe6ce13eda715af78f61d1543b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$36+16+3=\\boxed{\\textbf{(C)} ~55}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"185\" height=\"30\" />\n</p><p>~mathboy282\n</p><p>Note: This solution is kind of a fakesolve because it assumes the hexagon is rotationally symmetric, which we can see it is not by the diagram in Solution 1.\n</p><p>~Almora\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28Solution_1_But_More_Condensed.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(Solution_1_But_More_Condensed)\">Solution 3 (Solution 1 But More Condensed)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (Solution 1 But More Condensed)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Since it is an equiangular hexagon, each interior angle measures <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/a/aaa4de95cc6371d125d5cf773cdd9f9913dd1d8e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$120$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" /> degrees, and by angle chasing you can conclude that all the triangles are equilateral. Since the area formula for an equiangular triangle is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/3/9639d29c1d1db502c536ef3fe59ab4cc5bc84d6e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s^2 \\frac{\\sqrt3}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"46\" height=\"43\" />, the side lengths of the triangles are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/5/ce5d3cbb9ab0a992ed503c2d4499e4f4af2f9d65.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$36$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/0/f10b04e9a3e940e63267d83412bda96aacd4dce8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$16 \\sqrt{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"43\" height=\"18\" />. In the above diagram the sum of the lengths of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/d/c9daba06f5ec9c5069f24bbeab9c7151ab4f68c2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AF$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/b/52b3b006e6f9fe1681bd1f340795f789d888318e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$FE$\" width=\"28\" height=\"12\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/5/ce5d3cbb9ab0a992ed503c2d4499e4f4af2f9d65.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$36$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> and the sum of the lengths of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/4/0948661e822f2a953c43a57ac9e40b2734476de4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/5/6d5c9afa92ae3180f02754c177e530d9d0f8b4d4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$DE$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/0/580fa51087efd01969f8ad190ace7038c99fd137.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$16\\sqrt{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"43\" height=\"18\" />. Since these are also the side lengths of the hexagon, the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc16779de9fdcc83d6b31cb0db012dc1ef2a2111.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$36+16+3=55  (C)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"163\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p><p>~NamyaB.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_by_OmegaLearn_.28Angle_Chasing_and_Equilateral_Triangles.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_OmegaLearn_(Angle_Chasing_and_Equilateral_Triangles)\">Video Solution by OmegaLearn (Angle Chasing and Equilateral Triangles)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by OmegaLearn (Angle Chasing and Equilateral Triangles)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/ptBwDcmDaLA\">https://youtu.be/ptBwDcmDaLA</a>\n</p><p>~ pi_is_3.14\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_TheBeautyofMath\">Video Solution by TheBeautyofMath</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by TheBeautyofMath\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/8qcbZ8c7fHg\">https://youtu.be/8qcbZ8c7fHg</a>\n</p><p>~IceMatrix\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_10A\" title=\"2021 AMC 10A\">2021 AMC 10A</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_10A_Problems\" title=\"2021 AMC 10A Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_10A_Answer_Key\" title=\"2021 AMC 10A Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c13\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_20\" title=\"2021 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 20\">Problem 20</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_22\" title=\"2021 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 22\">Problem 22</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2021 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2021 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index